zink: one I/O variable per varying slot

rework_io_vars now creates one variable per varying slot, from component
0 up to the highest component accessed, instead of separate variables
for non-overlapping component ranges (fb_fetch and dual-source outputs
stay separate). A split slot, e.g. fixed-function texturing reading
TEX0.xy and TEX0.w for projective lookups while the vertex shader writes
TEX0 as vec4, becomes two fragment inputs in SPIRV-Cross's MSL. Metal
matches every fragment input to a vertex output by name, so MoltenVK's
pipeline creation failed and zink bound a NULL pipeline (crash). Vulkan
itself allows the split. Components below the first accessed one are
declared but unused.

Upstream: not submitted; Mesa 26 replaces this code with
nir_unlower_io_to_vars, which uses the same policy.

--- a/src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_compiler.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/zink/zink_compiler.c
@@ -5251,11 +5251,11 @@
       return;
 
    /* filter ignored components to scan later:
-    * - ignore no-overlapping-components case
+    * - non-overlapping components share the slot's variable (see create_io_var)
     * - always match fbfetch and dual src blend
     */
    if (ris->component_mask &&
-       (!(ris->component_mask & cmask) || ris->fb_fetch_output != sem.fb_fetch_output || ris->dual_source_blend_index != sem.dual_source_blend_index)) {
+       (ris->fb_fetch_output != sem.fb_fetch_output || ris->dual_source_blend_index != sem.dual_source_blend_index)) {
       ris->ignored_component_mask |= cmask;
       return;
    }
@@ -5372,6 +5372,11 @@
 {
    char name[1024];
    assert(ris->component_mask);
+   /* one variable per slot from component 0 on, like nir_unlower_io_to_vars (Mesa 26): SPIRV-Cross turns variables
+    * at component offsets into separate MSL interface members (user(locnN_C)), which Metal cannot link to a
+    * vertex output covering the whole slot (MoltenVK pipeline creation fails)
+    */
+   ris->component_mask = BITFIELD_MASK(util_last_bit(ris->component_mask));
    if (ris->newname || ris->name) {
       snprintf(name, sizeof(name), "%s", ris->newname ? ris->newname : ris->name);
    /* always use builtin name where possible */
